messages:
maria (DevEx): Heads up — the new JS sample app in the quickstart is failing for Next.js Edge builds. Getting a runtime error: "require is not defined" when importing the SDK.

sam (SDK): Oof. That sounds like the sample is using CommonJS require in an ESM/Edge context. Can you paste the top of the file?

maria (DevEx): Sure — here's the snippet from pages/api/rdw.ts in the sample app:```js
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = Redwood.createClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
```

noah (edge-eng): Yup — Edge runtimes (Vercel Edge, Cloudflare Workers) don't support dynamic require. The SDK publishes an ESM build but the sample is using the CJS style. For Next Edge you need to use import.

sam (SDK): Right. The runtime-compatible pattern is:```js
import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference/edge';
const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY });
export default async function handler(req) { /* ... */ }
```
We ship a separate entrypoint for edge to keep bundle size down and avoid node-only polyfills.

maria (DevEx): That makes sense. The docs quickstart uses the top-level package, which apparently re-exports CJS and ESM variants. I think the sample author defaulted to require out of habit.

jen (Docs): I can patch the quickstart. Two clarifications I'll add to the guide: 1) For Edge runtimes use `@redwood/inference/edge` ESM entrypoint; 2) If your app is CommonJS (server/node), use the CJS import form. I'll link examples for both.

sam (SDK): Also calling out TS typings — if you use `import Redwood from '@redwood/inference'` and then try `.createClient` you might hit: "Property 'createClient' does not exist on type 'typeof import(...)'" because of ambiguous default vs named exports. Recommended TS patterns:```ts
import { createClient } from '@redwood/inference';
// or for default compatibility:
import * as Redwood from '@redwood/inference';
const client = (Redwood as any).createClient(...); // last resort
```
We'll add a short section about named vs default in the SDK README.

noah (edge-eng): Also watch bundlers: With Vite/ESBuild you sometimes need to force the ESM resolve. Example in next.config.js for Next Edge functions:```js
module.exports = {
  experimental: { runtime: 'edge' },
  webpack: (config) => {
    config.resolve.mainFields = ['browser', 'module', 'main'];
    return config;
  },
};
```
That helped one of our customers.

maria (DevEx): One more small thing — the sample used `REDWOOD_API_KEY` in env but the docs show `REDWOOD_KEY` in one place. CI failed with 401. ci-bot posted the failing log: @ci-bot 

ci-bot: Failing test: quickstart-edge-imports — runtime error "require is not defined" and 401 for auth due to missing env var. Full logs: https://ci.example/quickstart/1234

sam (SDK): For retry behavior: Edge users expect fast retries on transient network errors, but not for non-idempotent ops. We default to 3 retries for idempotent GET-like endpoints (embeddings, health) and 0 for non-idempotent generation unless the client opts in. We should document that and show how to set a custom retry strategy:```js
const client = createClient({
  apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY,
  retry: { retries: 5, backoffMs: 200 }
});
```

jen (Docs): I'll add a short auth matrix and a note on retries + example code for both ESM and CJS in the quickstart. Opening PR now. https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/786

maria (DevEx): Awesome — thanks y'all. After the PR lands I'll re-run the sample app CI and bump the quickstart release. :party_parrot:
